\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {V}{\ignorespaces Checkpoint Progression, STBV-Bench v2.5b, Direct Classifier ($n{=}10{,}098$)}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:v25b}{{V}{7}{}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Every category's median confidence is nearly identical -- the real difference is how many individual messages are genuinely ambiguous.} Per-message B3 confidence distribution, Full STBV Framework, STBV-Bench v2.5b, current final checkpoint, grouped into the four semantic-attack categories Section~\ref {sec:problem} already defines (no new category invented, \texttt  {ATTACK\_VISUALIZATION\_REDESIGN.md}); recall (0.997-1.000 per category) and mean confidence alone were both rejected as visualization metrics for being near-constant. Boxes show real quartiles; points are real individual messages, plotted whenever more than 1.5$\times $ IQR from the box (standard convention) -- not a display artifact. Multi-Source Manipulation has 0 of 817 messages below $\tau _H{=}0.70$ (a single, tight cluster); Authority Claims 22/1,706 (1.3\%); Narrative Manipulation 47/1,596 (2.9\%); Indirection 35/1,245 (2.8\%). The two categories with the most low-confidence outliers -- Narrative Manipulation and Indirection -- attack by reframing or displacing intent across a sentence rather than asserting one concentrated, checkable false claim (an authority claim, a fabricated cross-source agreement), a harder discrimination problem for a semantic classifier and exactly the case the Trust Decision Engine's floor rules route to Caution rather than a forced binary call.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig_attack_family_v25b}{{3}{8}{}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The architecture almost never misses an attack, and its entire remaining cost is precision.} \emph  {Left:} confusion matrix, Full STBV Framework, STBV-Bench v2.5b, current final checkpoint ($n{=}10{,}098$): FN=6 (0.1\% of 5,364 real attacks slip through) against FP=1,491 (31.5\% of 4,734 benign messages over-flagged) -- the asymmetry is the paper's central deployment trade-off, not an incidental number: 99.6\% of all errors are this over-cautious false-positive type, not an attack getting through, so a deployment reading this correctly should tune for recall headroom, not worry about attacks silently passing. Chosen as the single confusion matrix retained in this manuscript (Table~\ref {tab:v25b} carries all configurations' exact metrics; per-family recall is uniform at 0.99--1.00, so a family-level breakdown would repeat this same picture 13 times). \emph  {Right:} the same real decisions as a distribution (63\% Reject, 32\% Accept, 4\% Caution) -- Reject-heavy is expected, not a miscalibration: v2.5b is 53\% attack-labeled by construction and the architecture's floor rules are conservative by design (Proposition~1), so genuine attacks and over-cautious benign flags both land in Reject.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig_v25b_confusion_grid}{{4}{8}{}{figure.4}{}}
